WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
My mantra on addiction is, "Not why
the addiction, but why the pain."

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:06.000
If you want to look at what causes
addiction, you have to look at

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:07.400
the benefit of addiction...

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:17.720
People say, typically, "it gave me
pain relief", "escape from stress",

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.680
"it gave me a sense of connection",
"a sense of belonging."

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.760
The addiction met some essential
human need that otherwise

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:26.360
wasn't met in that person's life.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.720
So all of these states of lacking
connection or being isolated

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.600
of having pain or having
too much stress in your life,

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.240
these are states of emotional pain.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:38.880
And when you look populations
of addicts, what you find is that

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:42.960
the more adversity in childhood,
exponentially the greater

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:44.120
the risk of addiction.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.480
Which doesn’t mean that every person
traumatised will become an addict,

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.320
but it does mean that
every addict was traumatised.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.520
In my opinion addiction is manifested
in any behaviour that a person finds

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.480
temporary pleasure or relief in,
but suffers negative consequences

00:00:59.560 --> 00:01:03.120
as a result of, and does not give up
or cannot give up,

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.800
despite those negative consequences.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.720
Tobacco, alcohol,
substances of all kinds.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:14.720
It could also be related to sex,
gambling, shopping, eating, work.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:19.040
Virtually any area of human activity
can become addictive

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.040
depending on the person's
relationship to it.

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:23.720
I had two major addictions
in my life.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:29.560
One was to work, and I also
had an addiction to shopping,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:33.120
in my case for classical music CDs.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.840
One day I spent $8,000
on compact discs.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.680
It doesn't matter how many sets of
a particular composer's symphonies

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:42.720
you already have, you have to get
the next one and the next one.

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:46.400
In the grip of this shopping fever,
I once left a patient in labour

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.560
and actually went downtown to get
the disc and I missed the delivery.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.640
That’s how impactful it was.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:56.240
Now you may think that's laughable,
"How could you compare your addiction

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.200
to those of your
heroin-addicted patients?"

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.920
My own addicted patients,
when I told them about my addictions

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.640
they didn’t laugh or they shook their
heads and they said,

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.280
"Yeah Doc, we get it.
You're just like the rest of us."

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:07.520
Well the point is that
we're all just like the rest of us.

00:02:07.720 --> 00:02:11.720
The greatest myths on addiction,
number one, is that it's genetic.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.920
It does run in families,
but why does it run in families?

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:19.280
If I'm an alcoholic, and if I yell
and scream at my kids

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.880
and then they grew up to soothe
themselves with alcohol,

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.040
did I pass it on to them genetically?
Or is that a behaviour they

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:28.280
developed because I recreated the
same conditions that I grew up in.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:32.000
Now there might be genetic
predispositions, but a predisposition

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:35.160
is not the same
as a predetermination.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:38.440
The other myth around addiction is
that it's a choice that people make.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:41.920
And the whole legal system is based
on the idea that people are choosing

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.440
to be addicts, lets punish them
for it so as to deter others.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:47.720
Addiction is not a choice
anybody makes,

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.160
it's a response to emotional pain.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.760
The other myth is that addiction is
restricted to the substance user

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.720
or to a few losers in our society.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.400
It's rife and rampant
through our culture.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:03.600
You would think that, with the utter
failure of most treatment modalities

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.800
when it comes to addiction, we would
wake up and ask ourselves,

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:09.000
"Do we really understand
this condition?"

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:14.560
But that doesn’t seem to happen.
We’re not looking at its real nature

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:18.720
as a response to human suffering
because we're not helping people

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:22.320
work through and resolve
their traumas.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.120
So we keep saying,
"What's wrong with you?"

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.520
Instead of asking,
"What happened to you?"

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:30.140
Thanks for watching.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.460
